---
import Answer from "../../components/challenge/Answer.astro";
import ChallengeIntro from "../../components/challenge/ChallengeIntro.astro";
import Checklist from "../../components/challenge/Checklist.astro";
import Message from "../../components/challenge/Message.astro";
import Task from "../../components/challenge/Task.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Accessibility Review - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="exploring-ai-capabilities" subChapterId="accessibility-review">
		<ChallengeIntro skill="For a broad, repetitive review, fan the work out to sub-agents on a cheaper, faster model, and keep the expensive model for pulling the results together.">
			Kaiju Mutual needs an accessibility review of all three apps before its next release. You'll have the agent check every
			page, fix what's quick, and report the rest.
		</ChallengeIntro>

		<Message kind="email" subject="Accessibility review before the release" from="Head of Digital" to="Web Team" date="Tue, 6 Oct 2026 08:40">
			<p>
				A policyholder who uses a screen reader wrote in to say they couldn't finish a claim. Legal wants a full accessibility
				review of the website and both portals before the next release.
			</p>
			<p>
				Fix anything quick and safe. For anything that changes how a page looks or works, give us a list so design and product
				can weigh in. Please send it as one report we can pass around.
			</p>
		</Message>

		<Task>Write a prompt to have your agent review every page of the apps for accessibility, using sub-agents on a cheaper model.</Task>

		<Checklist
			items={[
				"Cover every page of the website, the consumer portal and the admin portal.",
				"Hand the pages out to sub-agents running on a cheaper, faster model.",
				"Test the running apps in the browser, not just the source code.",
				"Fix the simple issues and mark them as fixed.",
				"List the issues that need a design or behavior change for the team to decide, without changing them.",
				"Say what an automated check can't cover and still needs a person.",
				"Produce one HTML report covering everything.",
			]}
		/>

		<Answer
			prompt={`Complete an accessibility review of the website, the consumer portal and the admin portal against WCAG 2.2 AA. Check each page in agent-browser as well as in the code.

Fix any clear, simple issues now and mark them as fixed in the report. For anything that would change functionality or design, list it with the page, the problem and a suggested fix, so we can get feedback from the team. Produce one HTML report for everything when done.

To get this done quickly, fan out one sub-agent per page, running on a cheaper, faster model such as Sonnet.`}
		>
			<p>
				A per-page check is narrow and repetitive, so a smaller model handles it well and costs less. The main agent, on the
				stronger model, merges the findings, removes duplicates and writes the report.
			</p>
			<p>
				In Claude Code you can name the sub-agent model in the prompt, or set <code>model</code> in a sub-agent's definition
				under <code>.claude/agents/</code>. In Cursor, set <code>model</code> in the frontmatter of a custom sub-agent under{" "}
				<code>.cursor/agents/</code>. Some of Cursor's built-in sub-agents pick their own model.
			</p>
			<p>
				Expect two quick fixes. The peril picker on the new claim form and the plan picker on the new quote page have radio
				buttons with no accessible name, so a screen reader reads out five identical "radio" items. That's the claim the
				policyholder couldn't finish. The labels are in the code, but the browser never connects them, so an agent that only
				reads the source usually misses it. The sidebar menu in both portals also isn't inside a landmark.
			</p>
			<p>
				Fix the radios where they're used. Rewriting the shared components in <code>components/ui</code> goes further than the
				task needs.
			</p>
			<p>
				Expect three contrast issues for the team: light text on the siren orange, teal on grey-green forecast badges, and faded
				inactive tabs on the claims pages. The orange is the brand's alarm color, so the agent should list it, not recolor it.
			</p>
			<p>
				A good report also says what a scan can't check: keyboard flow, focus in the quote modal and dialogs, toast
				announcements, zoom, and text over the home page's gradients and 3D scene.
			</p>
		</Answer>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
